kai: Quick investigation note 1: reproducible issue uploading multipart files from Android SDK with Retrofit/OkHttp when a redirect or automatic retry happens. The second attempt ends up with an empty/truncated body and server returns 400. Repro steps below.

kai: Steps to repro: 1) Use sampleapp file-upload endpoint that streams a large file via RequestBody (streaming). 2) Force a 307 redirect on the first upload (test proxy). 3) OkHttp follows redirect and retries; the reissued Call has an exhausted RequestBody and server rejects it.

sophie: Are you using RequestBody that reads from InputStream directly? Those are not repeatable. Which code path in the SDK constructs the body?

kai: SDK builds the multipart with a streaming RequestBody for memory reasons (avoid full-file buffer). Code simplified: ```kotlin
val rb = object: RequestBody() {
  override fun contentType() = "application/octet-stream".toMediaType()
  override fun writeTo(sink: BufferedSink) {
    inputStream.source().use { src -> sink.writeAll(src) }
  }
}
val part = MultipartBody.Part.createFormData("file", filename, rb)
service.upload(part)
```

rahul: That writeTo consumes the stream once. OkHttp can only replay body if it knows contentLength and can re-create the body or if request is buffered. Redirects and some retries create a new Call and need a repeatable body.

sophie: Short-term workaround: buffer the body in memory or temp file so the RequestBody can be recreated on retry. For example: `RequestBody.create(mediaType, file.readBytes())` or wrap as `RequestBody.create(mediaType, file)` which provides length.

kai: We tried buffering with `.readBytes()` and that fixed the problem but memory usage spikes for large files. Not ideal for mobile.

rahul: Other options: 1) disable automatic follow redirects / retries for endpoints that use streaming bodies (OkHttpClient.Builder().followRedirects(false).retryOnConnectionFailure(false) for these calls), 2) implement a RequestBody that can re-open the InputStream on each writeTo (e.g., by holding a Supplier<InputStream> that opens a fresh stream), 3) change server to respond with 308 (permanent redirect) and accept resumable uploads.

sophie: Implementing a Supplier-based RequestBody is reasonable: ensure the supplier opens a new FileInputStream for each writeTo and return contentLength when possible. Example: ```kotlin
class RewindableFileRequestBody(val file: File, val media: MediaType) : RequestBody() {
  override fun contentType() = media
  override fun contentLength() = file.length()
  override fun writeTo(sink: BufferedSink) {
    FileInputStream(file).source().use { src -> sink.writeAll(src) }
  }
}
```

kai: That worked in my tests and avoids reading whole file into memory. For streams that are truly ephemeral (camera stream) we need to either buffer to a temp file or mark the request non-retryable.

rahul: Action items: 1) Add a note to mobile onboarding docs about "non-repeatable request bodies" and recommended patterns (owner: sophie), 2) Add an SDK helper factory for RewindableFileRequestBody and a convenience flag to disable follow redirects/retries for streaming endpoints (owner: kai), 3) Add CI test that forces a 307 redirect and asserts upload succeeds or errors clearly (owner: rahul).

build-bot: Created ticket sdk-android#558 "multipart upload: streaming RequestBody can't be replayed on redirect/retry" and attached logs (assigned to @kai).

kai: I'll open a small PR with RewindableFileRequestBody + docs snippet and default the SDK to mark endpoints that call `upload()` as non-retryable unless user opts in. Thanks for the quick triage. :+1:
